WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.000
Leading responsible AI use in your team.

2
00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:06.630
This is the video for module 3 of AI Literacy.

3
00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:11.440
Once you can use AI critically, others will ask for help.

4
00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:17.080
The wrong response is to start with tools. The right response is to start with their work.

5
00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:23.620
You will learn to find the tasks where AI truly helps, and the ones that must stay human.

6
00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:29.610
You will learn to cost a workshop, so helping others does not drain your own pocket.

7
00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:36.170
And you will learn to write a short AI use policy that people can follow on their phones.

8
00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:45.190
Discovery means understanding what people actually do each week. Ask which written or repeated tasks take the most time.

9
00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.490
Ask who reads the result, and what happens if it contains a mistake.

10
00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:58.890
Mark every task that touches personal data. These need anonymisation rules and a named person accountable.

11
00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:06.770
Then score each task from 1 to 5 for value, meaning time saved, and for risk, meaning harm if wrong.

12
00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:11.710
High value and low risk tasks are the best first projects.

13
00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:15.200
High-risk tasks stay with people, whatever their value.

14
00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:28.200
For example, Mrs Okon owns a secondary school in Uyo with 14 teachers. Chioma, an AI literacy graduate, interviews her and three teachers and scores six tasks.

15
00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:35.360
First drafts of lesson notes score value 5 and risk 2. Start here, with teacher review.

16
00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:40.600
Practice quiz questions from the scheme of work are also a good start.

17
00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:48.120
Report card comments are higher risk, so they are only a pilot, with no student names pasted in.

18
00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:55.880
Automatic answers to parents' fee questions are delayed, because wrong answers about money damage trust.

19
00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:05.050
And deciding which students repeat a class stays fully human. Sometimes the honest answer is that AI is not the fix.

20
00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:17.220
Even unpaid work has costs, so cost before you price. For example, Folake is asked to run a one-day workshop for 20 members of a market women's association.

21
00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:27.920
She counts 14 hours for preparation, delivery and follow-up, and values her time at 5,000 naira an hour. That is 70,000 naira.

22
00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:38.530
Printing is about 8,000 naira, transport about 6,000 and data about 3,000. Her direct costs are 17,000 naira.

23
00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:48.790
The total is 87,000 naira, or 4,350 naira per participant. She quotes 90,000 naira for the day.

24
00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:53.250
If the group cannot pay, she knows exactly what to ask a sponsor for.

25
00:02:54.500 --> 00:03:02.800
Write a short proposal: purpose, deliverables, exclusions, responsibilities, timeline, price, and how success will be checked.

26
00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:07.870
Promise skills you can measure, not claims that AI will double productivity.

27
00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:17.670
When someone asks for more, like a Facebook page or a grant, check it against the proposal. If it is out of scope, offer a separate price.

28
00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:24.930
Take payment by traceable transfer and confirm it in your account app. Screenshots of transfers can be faked.

29
00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:33.570
A one-page policy prevents leaked data and confusion. Start with its purpose, and which official apps are approved.

30
00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:39.470
List the allowed uses, like first drafts, summaries and training materials.

31
00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:47.710
List what is not allowed: decisions on hiring, discipline or admissions, and medical or legal advice.

32
00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:56.710
Set data rules. No names, phone numbers, ID numbers, bank details or health information. Anonymise first.

33
00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:05.620
Name a person who checks every fact before anything goes out. Say when AI helped, and review the policy every six months.

34
00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:16.960
Never create or share AI images, video or voice of a real person without their clear permission. It can cause serious harm and may break the law.

35
00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:28.940
If someone breaks the policy, respond calmly. The aim is to learn, not to shame. Find out what happened, correct it, and tell anyone who received wrong information.

36
00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:38.890
If other people's personal data was exposed, tell the person responsible for data protection. Then record the incident and the fix.

37
00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:52.860
To remember. Start with the work, not the tools. Keep high-risk decisions human. Cost your time and every expense. Agree scope and payment in writing. And give your team a one-page policy.

38
00:04:54.110 --> 00:05:07.160
Now run a mini discovery, cost one support package, and draft your policy. Then write up one portfolio case with your prompt, check record and final version, including an AI error you caught.

39
00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:09.450
Continue with the lessons below.
